## 1. Introduction
Atmospheric particulate matter (PM) pollution is one of the leading global security issues, which has caused severe environmental problems and significant economic losses [1]. Particulate matter (PM) combines tiny solid particles and droplets in the air, including organic carbon, elemental carbon, other organic matter, nitrates, sulfates, and inorganic substances [2]. Due to its small size, PM2.5 can penetrate the respiratory tract and blood vessels. Many data suggest that prolonged exposure to PM2.5 leads to various respiratory and cardiovascular diseases [3]. Air purification technology (filter mask and air purifier) can prevent personal contact with PM2.5. However, PM2.5 captured on the filter surface may still have deleterious properties due to its potentially toxic components without subsequent treatment. Moreover, the potential danger of human exposure to PM2.5 when cleaning or repairing filters [4]. Therefore, developing an air filter film that can effectively capture and green to degrade PM2.5 is crucial to improving air pollution.
The nanofiber membrane produced by electrospinning can effectively solve the filtration problem of PM2.5, which mainly depends on the outstanding advantages of small fiber diameter, high specific surface area, good internal connectivity, and morphology control [5]. Therefore, many polymers produced by electrospinning are used to design fiber membranes as air filters, including polyethylene (PE), polyacrylonitrile (PAN), polyethylene liquid, polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF), polyethylene (PI), polyurethane (PU), polypropylene (PP), poly tetrafluoroethylene (PTFE), etc [6-14]. These conventional membranes have a drawback because they are prone to clog during use, leading to a rapid decline in filtration performance. These shortcomings greatly limit the applicability of the conventional membrane [16].
Ideally, particle filtration films have excellent mechanical properties to collect large particles with minimal pressure drop. To achieve the desired properties, the membrane shall have the following properties: (1) High specific surface area, the unit membrane surface can deal with a large amount of gas; (2) It has a smaller pore size than particles filtered by the interception or with high affinity, allowing for filtration by inertial shock, diffusion, and electrostatic mechanisms; (3) Porosity connectivity, high pore, low pore ratio;(4) Long-term stability of the membrane [18].
MOFs are coordination polymers composed of inorganic metals and organic ligands that have recently been widely used in sensing [19,20], airflow breakaway [21,22] catalysis [23], and gas storage [24]. The large surface area $(>1500~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ , an adjustable aperture, and a high pore volume make it ideal for filtering PM2.5 [25]. In addition to being filtered by interception mechanisms, these materials can improve filtration performance by absorbing particles and other harmful gaseous contaminants [26]. Furthermore, the MOFs are designed to bind to different photocatalysts and are considered to be the hybrid structures of the semiconductor-MOF [19]. Some of these unique physical and chemical properties may originate from synergistic effects caused by the semiconductor-MOF hybrid structure. Under these conditions, the photodegradation efficiency of MOF and semiconductor is improved [27].
In this study, PTZ membranes with microscale $/$ nanoscale hierarchical structures were prepared using a two-step growth method. First, the 2-methimazole ligands were dispersed on the PT fibers by electrospinning. Then, the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion in the hexahydrate zinc nitrate solution coordinates with 2-methimazole. Second, the $\operatorname{PT}@2$ -methimidazole membrane was immersed in the ZIF-8 seed solution, and a layer of ZIF-8 nanocrystals was grown uniformly on the fiber surface. The prepared PTZ membrane has excellent air filtration capacity and photocatalytic activity. The results show that coating the ZIF-8 nanocrystal in the PT membrane microfiber membrane improves the filtration efficiency of PM2.5 (from $70\%$ to $99\%$ and the photocatalytic efficiency (from $66\%$ to $85\%$ . The nitrogen gas $\left(\mathsf{N}_{2}\right)$ adsorption analyzer and the Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy results show that the improved PM2.5 filtration efficiency may be due to the large specific surface area and polar functional groups. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and photoluminescence (PL) data show enhanced PM2.5 photodegradation associated with the N-Ti-O bond, which is thought to reduce the recombination of electron-hole pairs. The prepared PTZ membranes are promising for the collection and green degradation of PM2.5.
## 2. Experimental sections
## 2.1. Materials
Polyacrylonitrile (PAN, MW $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ 150,000); N, Ndimethylformamide (DMF); $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (P25, $20\ \mathrm{nm}$ ); Isopropanol (IPA); Triethanolamine (TEOA); Qhenylquinone (BQ) and Methanol were purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd., China. 2- Methylene imidazole (2-MI); Hydrate zinc nitrate $\left(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})\right.$ $\mathsf{6H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\dot{}.$ ) and Acetonitrile were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Company. All chemicals were of analytical grade and used without further purification.
## 2.2. Preparation of PT/2-MI membrane
Soluble PAN $(2.0{\ g})$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}(0.5\:\mathrm{g})$ , and 2-MI (1.0 g) were dispersed in $_{11\mathrm{~g~}}$ of DMF by magnetic agitation, and then the solution was electrospinning into a membrane at $20\mathrm{kV}$ . Then, the prepared film was dried at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ to expel the excess solvent.
PAN $(2.00\ \mathrm{g})$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(0.5~\mathrm{g})$ were dissolved in $11.00{\mathrm{g}}{}$ of DMF and stirred evenly to prepare the spinning precursor. Prepared according to the above process $\mathrm{PAN@TiO_{2}}$ membrane, referred to as PT.
## 2.3. Preparation of PTZ membrane
First, zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(0.3\ \mathrm{g})$ was added to the metha$\mathrm{nol}(10\mathrm{ml})$ and magnetically stirred for $10\mathrm{min}$ . After that, $_{0.5\mathrm{~g~}}$ of 2-MI was increased to the mixed solution and magnetically stirred for $15{\mathrm{~min}}$ . The PT/2-MI membrane was soaked in ZIF-8 growth solution for $1\ \mathrm{min}$ , and then, the membrane was slowly washed with deionized water. In the end, the washed membrane was dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf C.$
In this process, the ZIF-8 growth solution was expressed as Z-1, and the manufactured membrane was described as PTZ-1. By reducing the content of $\mathrm{{Zn}(N O_{3}){\bullet}G H_{2}O}$ and 2-MI, the Z-0.5 and PTZ-0.5 were prepared. In the same way, the Z-2 and PTZ-2 were designed by redoubling the content of $\mathrm{~Zn~}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})\bullet6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and 2-MI.
## 2.4. Particulate matter (PM) filtration measurement
To test the performance of PTZ, a test bench with an area of $16\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ was established (Fig. 2). Moxa sticks are burned to produce PM2.5, and a blower delivers steady air at a speed of $2~\mathrm{m}/\mathrm{s}$ . Two PM2.5 sensors were used to gauge the upstream and downstream PM2.5 contents to calculate the PM2.5 filtration efficiency. All experimental data are the average values of 5 repetitions. The filtration efficiency $(E)$ and pressure drop of the membrane were calculated from Eqs. (1) and (2), respectively [31].
$$
E=\left(\frac{C0-C}{C0}\right)\times100\%
$$
where $c$ is the contents of PM upstream, and $\boldsymbol{C0}$ is the contents of PM downstream.
$$
\Delta P=P1-P2
$$
where $A P$ is the pressure drop, and P1 and P2 are the air-flow pressures of the upper and lower sections of the membrane.
The quality factor (QF) is considered a comprehensive parameter used to evaluate filtration material’s filtration performance [32].
$$
Q F=-\frac{l n(1-E)}{\Delta P}
$$
where $E$ and $A P$ are the filtration efficiency and pressure drop of the membrane.
## 2.5. 5. Characterization
The membrane’s surface morphology was observed by scanning electron microscope (SEM, Hitachi S4800). The crystal structures of ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ were studied by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Shimadzu XRD-6000). Fourier determined the functional groups on the surface of PTZ-1mwmbrane to transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR, TENSOR37, Germany). Nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms were absorbed and collected by an Autosorb-iQ-C(BET). The chemical state of the PTZ-1 surface was examined with X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Escalab 250Xi). The UV–visible diffuse reflectance spectrum (DRS) samples were measured by a UV–visible spectrophotometer (Hitachi U-4100) with a wavelength range of $200{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The model photoluminescence (PL) spectrum was detected with a Hitachi F4600 fluorescence spectrophotometer. The labrum HR Evolution spectrometer obtained Raman spectra, and a helium–neon laser $(632.8~\mathrm{nm})$ ) was used to excite the samples. The GC–MS system utilized is a Thermo Scientific Trace 1310 GC (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) coupled to an IQS single quadrupole MS.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Filtration performance of PTZ membrane
The morphological changes of PZT were studied by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). As shown in Fig. $3(\mathsf{a}_{1},\mathsf{a}_{2})$ , the PT have a smooth surface intertwined to form a reticular structure, which can effectively capture PM2.5. By contrast, the outside of PTZ nanofiber was evenly coated with ZIF-8 crystals. Although the spatial structure was effectively retained, the surface topography was markedly altered. The nano/ microstructure of PTZ was created due to the coverage of ZIF-8 crystals (Fig. 3c1- c2). The diam of the nanofibrous also swelled from 408 to $589~\mathrm{nm}$ . The morphology of the PTZ nanofibrous can be tuned by changing the concentration of the ZIF-8 growth solution. (Fig. 3b1-d2).
The XRD diagrams of bare ZIF-8, PT and PTZ-1 are shown in Fig. 4(a). The tested PTZ-1 was a duplex structure and the composite peaks matched ZIF-8 and titanium dioxide. The peaks of 12.9,14.9, and 18.2 correspond to the (112), (022), and (222) crystal surfaces of the ZIF-8 [16]. In addition, the 25.4,38.1,48.0 and 53.9 peaks of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ correspond to the (101), (112), (200), and (105) planes of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , that is, the phase of each material is not affected during PTZ-1 synthesis, and the growth of ZIF-8 in PTZ-1 is successful. The Raman spectroscopy of ZIF-8PT and PTZ-1 are shown in Fig. 4(b). The Raman spectra of 140, 393, 512, and $635~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were tallied with the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The spectrum of ZIF-8 at $268~\mathrm{cm}^{-1},686~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1146~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1384~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to Zinc-N bonds, imidazole ring folding, $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ stretching, and methyl bending, respectively. All the peaks on the Raman spectra of PTZ1 coincided with ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , further confirming the growth of the ZIF-8 crystals on the fiber surface [33-35].
An essential parameter for the ability to capture PM2.5 is filtration efficiency. In this study, moxa sticks were selected as a source of PM2.5, an excellent model for air filtration because it includes an enormous number of smoke particles, and its composition is similar to PM2.5 particles. The as-prepared PTZ can be directly applied for PM2.5 filtration. As shown in Fig. 5(a), the filtration performance of PTZ with ZIF-8 on the surface was better than that of PT, and the filtration performance increased from $71\%$ to $90\%$ ， $99\%$ , and $99.2\%$ , respectively. In addition, the resistance pressure drops also rose from $22\mathsf{P a}$ to 30 Pa, $34\mathrm{Pa}$ , and $46\mathsf{P a}$ , respectively. Eqs(3) calculate the filtering materials’ QF, as shown in Fig. 5(b). Compared with PT $(0.056\ \mathsf{P a}^{-1},$ ), the QF of the PTZ-0.5, PTZ-1, and PTZ-2 increased to 0.076, 0.135, and $0.105\ \mathrm{Pa}^{-1}$ , respectively. The higher QF of the PTZ declares a better filtration feature.
The above research shows that the filtration performance of PTZ-1 is better than that of PT because of the different patterns of filtering PM2.5 by PT and PTZ-1. Fig. 6 explains the PM2.5 filtration performances of two nanofibrous. The PM2.5 captured on the nanofibrous exhibited different morphologies under the same filtration time. And PM2.5 particles were adsorbed asymmetrically in the PT nanofibrous, most of which were aggregated at the nodes of the nanofibrous, and a handful of particles appeared in the middle of the strings (Fig. 6a–b). PM2.5 particles were not only captured by PTZ-1 nanofibrous at the nodes but an enormous number of particles were captured on the fibers (Fig. 6c–d), which is different from PT nanofibrous. The primary filtering mechanism for PT capture PM2.5 is passive filtering, such as Brownian diffusion, direct interception, and inertial shocks [40]. However, due to the limited filtration efficiency, the mechanical filtration mechanism can only intercept large particles [41]. In addition to mechanical filtration, PTZ-1 also has activity-trapping properties.
Except for passive filtration, the PTZ-1 also has the following filtration mechanisms after cladding ZIF-8 on the surface: (1) The ZIF-8 surface contains many polar functional groups, as shown in Fig. 7(a). The infrared spectrum of the PTZ-1 presents a combination of ZIF-8, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and PAN spectra after adding ZIF-8. The adsorption of PM2.5 would be significantly affected by the abundant functional groups on the PTZ-1, which was attributed to the interaction between the functional groups between the PTZ-1 and PM2.5 [42]; (2) As shown in Fig. 7(b), the specific surface area of the PT was $12.93\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}.$ , and the specific surface area of PTZ-1 increased to $389.85~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . The specific surface area of the PTZ-1 after in situ growth was greatly improved due to the high specific surface area of ZIF-8 nanocrystals, which helped to improve the surface energy of the PTZ-1, thereby increasing the deposition probability of PM2.5 on the fiber surface [43]; (3) The enhanced ability of the PZT-1 to filter PM was also attributed to more vital electrostatic forces (Fig. 7c). The improved knowledge of the membrane to trap PMs is due to the positive charge provided by the unbalanced metal ions in this process [44,45]; (4) ZIF-8 contains a significant quantity cavities and abundant organic adsorption sites and uncovered metal sites (Fig. 7d), which enable the PTZ-1 to have excellent PMs adsorption capacity [46,47].
Fig. 8 shows the advantages of PTZ-1 compared with other filters under the same experimental conditions. The results show that the PTZ-1 has outstanding PM2.5 filtration performance and is higher than reported conventional and coated MOF air filters.
## 3.2. Photocatalytic degradation of PMs
In Fig. 7(a), the FT-IR spectrum shows that more polar functional groups are introduced into PTZ-1, and a new adsorption peak at $513\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ cans prove the formation of the $N$ -Ti-O bond [29]. During the agitation and pretreatment of the PT / 2-MI precursor solution, $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can be considered the binding site and act on the 2-MI of ZIF-8. The weak combining $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ and the 2-MI of ZIF-8 probably make N-Ti-O bonds. This specific structure may promote photocatalytic efficiency [48].
Fig. 9 shows the XPS spectra of valence states of various elements in PTZ-1. It can be seen from Fig. 9(a) that the binding energies of $Z\mathrm{n}2\mathrm{p}3/2$ and $2{\mathsf{p}}1/2$ of PTZ-1 are 1021.6 and 1044.7 eV. As shown in Fig. 9(b), the three $\textsf{N1s}$ peaks of 398.9 eV, 399.6 eV, and $400.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ are all composed of the ${\mathsf{N}}{=}{\mathsf{C}}$ , $N\mathrm{.}$ -Ti-O, and NAC bonds of the imidazole ring in ZIF-8. The $N$ -Ti-O bond indicates that part of the O atoms on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface is replaced by the N atoms in the 2-MI [49]. The comparison between Fig. 9(b) and Fig. 9(e) shows that the $N$ -Ti-O bond is unique to the PTZ-1 membrane and is not present in ZIF-8. At $530.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $523.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ the position of the O 1 s in PTZ-1 is shown in Fig. 9(c) due to the presence of lattice oxygen and surface hydroxyl groups in titanium dioxide. In the Ti 2p XPS spectrum (Fig. 9d), the binding energies of Ti
$2{\tt p}3/2$ and $2{\tt p}1/2$ were $458.7\mathrm{eV}$ and $464.4\:\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, indicating the presence of oxidized $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ in the PTZ-1 [50]. The Ti 2p1 $_{/2}$ and Ti $2{\tt p}3/2$ binding energy values of PTZ-1 were stronger than those found in the standard $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ samples Fig. 9(f), indicating an electron transfer in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to ZIF-8 [51]. This new hybrid structure can guide charge transfer and promote photogenerated electronhole separation [50].
To further understand the charge transfer properties of the composite material, we used photoluminescence (PL) to compare the carrier’s binding rates on the PT and PTZ membranes [52]. According to the photoluminescence theory, the higher the PL intensity, the faster the binding rate of photoluminescent carriers [53]. The PT showed a high photogenerated carriers recombination rate. At the same time, the PTZ-1 had a low PL intensity (Fig. 10a). By this reckoning, the PTZ-1 can promote the separation of charge carriers, suggesting a peculiar channel for carrier transport between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ZIF-8 [48].
The band gap of the sample is given by an $(\mathsf{a h v})^{2}$ versus absorbed light energy. Determine the $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}$ value by calculating the tangent’s X-intercept extending from the spectrums linear part. The band gaps for ZIF-8, PT, and PTZ-1 were 4.8,3.5, and $3.27\ \mathrm{eV}$ · respectively (Fig. 10b). In addition, the Eg value of the PTZ-1 helps to improve the photocatalytic efficiency. The $N$ -Ti-O bond promotes the separation of charge carriers, narrows the band gap of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and can widen the working range [48].
The filtered PTZ-1 and PT films were divided into $1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ fragments and degraded under UV irradiation. One fragment was removed every $30\ \mathrm{min}$ , dried, and soaked in $20\ \mathrm{ml}$ acetonitrile solution, followed by multiple sonication until the material adsorbed on the membrane was completely extracted. Finally, the supernatant was extracted by centrifugation. The extracted supernatant was detected using a UV–visible spectrophotometer, and the filtration efficiency was calculated by absorbance variation. The results showed that PTZ-1 has better photodegradation than PT. As shown in Fig. 11(a), the degradation performance of PZT-1 and PT increased from $68\%$ to $85\%$ within $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ after light.
Multiple trapping experiments were performed to explore further the significant active substances involved in PM2.5 degradation by PTZ-1 membranes. Isopropanol (IPA $20\ \mathrm{\mmol/L})$ , Triethanolamine (TEOA $20\ \mathrm{\mmol/L})$ , and Benzoquinone (BQ $1\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ were used as a scavenger for OH, $\mathrm{~H~}^{+}$ and $\cdot_{0}{}^{2-}$ , respectively. As shown in the Fig. 11(b), PTZ-1 degrades PM2.5 slightly after adding TEOA, and the degradation efficiency decreases by $30\%$ and $40\%$ after adding IPA and BQ. It can be inferred that OH and $\cdot_{0}{}^{2-}$ play a major role in the degradation process [51].
The organic components in PM particles are mostly polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs). Many studies have shown that the active site of PAHs is vulnerable to the attack of $\cdot_{0}{}^{2-}$ or OH active substances to form an open chain structure. Under the continuous action of the active substances, the long chain structure was divided into small molecules such as acids, ketones, and aldehydes and finally completely mineralized into ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ [54,55]. In this study, the degradation intermediates were analyzed by GC–MS (as shown in the Fig. 12 (a-c)), in which A was 1,7-diisopropylnaphthalene, B was 1,2-benzo carboxylic acid, bis (2- methylpropyl) ester, and c was Butanedioic acid, dibutyl ester. It can be inferred that B and C are ring-opening products of A. Then substance B and C is continuously affected by $\cdot_{0}{}^{2-}$ or OH and finally mineralized into $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ [56]. Since the intermediates described above account for only a small fraction of the intermediate products and the lifetimes of these products are extremely short, some intermediate products cannot be detected in GC–MS.
Based on the above discussion, the tentative mechanism of PTZ1 photocatalytic characteristics was proposed. The cooperation action of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ZIF-8 is essential to improve the photocatalytic activity of the PTZ-1 membrane. The generation and transformation of photogenerated carriers between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ZIF-8 surfaces are shown in Fig. 12(b). Firstly, the internal $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is the chief photo-catalyst, and the surface ZIF-8 can be used as a cocatalyst [57]. Secondly, the hybrid structure formed by ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is conducive to charge separation and band gap reduction [51]. The $\cdot_{0}{}^{2-}$ and OH were generated from the PTZ-1 can lead to the oxidative decomposition of PM2.5 [57]. In addition, the unique hybrid structure between ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ also accelerates the photodegradation of organic carbon in PM2.5 [59]. The green degradation of organic carbon in PM particles not only prolongs the service life of the PZT-1 but also reduces the harm of PM 2.5 filtration to the environment.
Fig. 13(a-b) shows the SEM images of PTZ-1 before and after photodegradation. While a lot of PM2.5 disappears after UV exposure, some remains on the fibers. The residual PM2.5 may involve inorganic sulfate, nitrate, metal oxide, and elemental carbon (inert to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ photolysis). In addition, no fiber fracture or collapse was observed after the photodegradation process, the structure of the fiber membrane remained stable, and PTZ could be recycled. The PTZ-1 membrane was subjected to multiple cycle filtration tests (as shown in the Fig. 13(c). After continuous filtration for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the filter membrane was placed under ultraviolet light for photodegradation and drying. The dried filter membrane undergoes the same test process of five times of filtration - photodegradation - drying; the filtration performance of PM2.5 changes from $98.85\%$ to $96.8\%$ After 5 cycles, the filtration performance and pressure drop are stable, indicating that PTZ-1 has good recoverability.
To test the long-term filtration stability of PTZ-1, the filter membrane was placed in the actual environment to test its air filtration performance for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . As shown in the Fig. 13(d), the filtration performance is stable after a constant decline in the first $24\mathrm{h}$ because the PM2.5 coated on the fiber surface in the early stage of filtration reduces the interaction between ZIF-8 and particles. At the same time, PM2.5 was adsorbed on the fiber surface will affect the air passage, and the resistance pressure drop of the PTZ-1 membrane increases by $4\ \mathrm{Pa}.$ . In the $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ filtration test, the filtration performance of the PTZ-1 membrane only decreased by $3.4\%$ and maintained a high filtration performance of $95.4\%$ , with good long-term filtration stability.
In addition to the above properties, the PTZ-1 also has good mechanical properties to meet the demands of practical use. As shown in Fig. 14(a), the stiffness of the PTZ-1 increased markedly at the inception phase of shape change, mainly because the orientation and shape change of the membrane were restricted by the ZIF-8 particles during stretching, and the improved stiffness also gave the membrane with support performance (Fig. 14b-c). The strain of the film also increased from $1.7\mathrm{MPa}$ to $2.4~\mathrm{MPa}$ . In addition, the PTZ-1 has high stiffness and maintains good flexibility.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, PTZ-1 nanofiber membrane with effective air filtration （ $(\mathrm{PM}2.5,99\%)$ and green degradation properties was prepared by electrospinning and in situ synthesis of MOF [16,18]. Compared with other air filtration membrane preparation methods recently reported, our simple synthesis method is more efficient to retain the integrity of ZIF-8 morphology. In the $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ filtration test, the filtration performance of the PTZ-1 membrane only decreased by $3.4\%$ and maintained a high filtration performance of $95.4\%$ , with good long-term filtration stability. It also has excellent photodegradation performance. The degradation efficiency of PM particles can reach $85\%$ under UV irradiation for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ The degraded membrane can be recycled and the filtration performance remains above $96\%$ after five cycles, greatly extending the service life of the membrane. Compared with PT membrane without ZIF-8, the filtration performance of PTZ-1 is improved by $28\%$ , and the degradation efficiency is improved by $20\%$ . The excellent comprehensive performance of PTZ-1 has broad application prospects in home protection and provides a new idea for developing multi-function filters for the future.
